00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.269
I love your whole outfit
man you look good.

00:00:27.269 --> 00:00:28.579
- Thank you, thank you.
- All right.

00:00:28.579 --> 00:00:29.412
With the jeans and everything.

00:00:29.412 --> 00:00:30.440
How you been?

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.138
- Good, busy.
- Good, busy?

00:00:32.138 --> 00:00:33.470
Busy, busy, busy.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:34.520
Artist of the Decade.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.340
- Yeah, it's been, that's pretty
wild, you know, when they--

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:38.173
- That was incredible.

00:00:38.173 --> 00:00:40.290
- The Academy of Country Music told me

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.920
that we were gonna be
the Artist of the Decade,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.269
and I kinda just felt like.

00:00:43.269 --> 00:00:44.928
(audience applauds)
Thank you!

00:00:44.928 --> 00:00:46.077
- It's amazing!

00:00:46.077 --> 00:00:47.768
- But it was kinda weird you know.

00:00:47.768 --> 00:00:50.680
I felt like I hadn't
been around long enough

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.020
to get something like that.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:52.920
And then I kinda turned around
- You've been around for--

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.370
- Well, you don't have say
how long I've been around,

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:57.790
but it was like, I looked
and it was like 14 years,

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.430
so I was like, well I
guess I have been around

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.420
for over a decade.
- [Kelly] Yeah, yeah.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.083
- But it was one of the biggest
honors of my career so far.

00:01:02.083 --> 00:01:04.360
- And you've had like the biggest songs,

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.117
you have the best catalog
and I was telling them,

00:01:06.117 --> 00:01:07.080
"You Make It Easy",

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.590
I know I wrote that in the
letter to you backstage,

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:11.110
I love that song.
- Wow, thank you, it was--

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.290
- Like I want my husband to slow dance

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.240
in the rain with me to that song.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.245
It is a sexy song y'all.

00:01:17.245 --> 00:01:19.740
(audience applauds)
It is a sexy song.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.730
So, here's my question though.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.330
We asked earlier though, and I know,

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:24.390
you know, Christmas coming up.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.530
What's the worst gift
you've ever received?

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:30.490
- The worst gift I think
was, a couple years ago,

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.750
I got a motion censored light that hangs

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.332
on your toilet, right?

00:01:35.332 --> 00:01:36.360
(audience laughs)

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.260
So when you stumble into the
bathroom at night it's dark,

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.910
this thing comes on and
it lights up the toilet

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:43.054
so you don't miss.

00:01:43.054 --> 00:01:44.360
- Oh, well that's thoughtful.
- Yeah.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.770
We moved and somehow it
came up missing, it was--

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:48.313
(laughs)
- That's amazing!

00:01:48.313 --> 00:01:50.843
- It was probably the
oddest gift I ever got.

00:01:50.843 --> 00:01:52.150
- That is amazing!
- Yeah.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:53.993
- I kinda wanna meet that person.

00:01:53.993 --> 00:01:56.450
You were on stage actually,

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.100
and this is more of a serious thing.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.650
But actually, I had friends
at the Vegas shooting,

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.510
they were Brian, actually
Brandon's best friend,

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:04.350
my husbands best friend.

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:05.960
He and his wife were there

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.370
at the Vegas shooting when that happened,

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:09.010
when you were doing your
show and everything.

00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:11.920
And I just, I know you just did a show,

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.200
in Vegas recently, like
to kind of, I don't know,

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.490
I guess it was, what was
the point of you doing that?

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:18.460
Because I--

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:20.740
Right when that happened and
our friends were literally

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.540
texting us during the shooting.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:23.763
And we were like, what?

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.630
It terrified me to ever go on stage

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:29.260
and I've been on stage forever.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.840
But it just scares you
'cause you're so vulnerable.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.930
- Yeah, I mean it's obviously something

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.930
that I wouldn't wish on anybody.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:40.330
And I think for us, going
back to Vegas was something

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.700
that I think we wanted
to do it the right way.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.840
We didn't wanna go in and
try and do it too soon.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.510
We felt like there was
gonna be a lot of people

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:51.980
at our show that probably
haven't been to one of our shows,

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:55.660
maybe haven't been to a concert
at all, since that show.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:56.810
And if they were gonna come back out,

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:59.960
and you know, that was the
healing process for them.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.950
You know, we wanted to make
it a safer environment,

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:04.460
something that made them feel safer.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:05.293
- Yeah.

00:03:05.293 --> 00:03:09.433
- So we did Park MGM and so you know,

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:12.980
little 5,000 seat arena that, you know,

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:16.390
the other one was outside,
30, 40,000 people,

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:17.223
whatever it was.

00:03:17.223 --> 00:03:20.150
And so this was a little
smaller, a little more intimate,

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.600
where people could come hopefully
feel a little more at ease

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.080
and hopefully help some of them.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.600
I mean, we had to kinda
get on stage the next week,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.939
you know, and start trying
to get through all of it.

00:03:30.939 --> 00:03:32.240
- How did you do that right off the bat?

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:33.460
Did you talk to anyone?

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:34.710
'Cause I'm telling you what, I--

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:36.610
- You know the best thing that happened--

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.186
- Especially having kids.
- Yeah.

00:03:38.186 --> 00:03:39.210
- You know what I'm saying,
like it's not just you

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:40.510
going out there, you have a family.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:42.230
- Yeah, I mean it was rough.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:44.530
I think the best thing that
could've happened was the fact,

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:46.930
not what could have happened,
but the best thing for me,

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:49.570
was the fact that my wife was
there and went through it.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:51.010
My band was there and went through it.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:52.078
My crew, everybody.

00:03:52.078 --> 00:03:54.400
And so we kinda leaned
on each other, you know,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.860
for that stuff and talked through it.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.690
You know nobody kinda kept it bottled up.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.560
I mean if you had a breakdown,

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:00.930
you kinda had it in front of everybody

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:02.450
and everybody knew what was going on.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:04.640
You know, my wife, you know we could talk

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:05.990
and she understood, you know,

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:07.850
what was going on because she was there.

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:09.270
- Yeah.
- And so it was just,

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.660
I think to me, that probably
helped more than anything.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.640
But you know, we're on
the other side of it now

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.580
and you know, we're part
of the Route 91 family now.

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:17.998
So it's a, it's cool group.

00:04:17.998 --> 00:04:20.038
- It is, it is, it's a good thing.

00:04:20.038 --> 00:04:21.070
(audience applauds)

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:24.090
So you're a busy dad, with
two little kids as well.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.090
This photo kinda sums it up, here we go.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:27.940
- Aww!
- Yeah (laughs).

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:29.050
- [Kelly] Now is that?

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:30.380
What's on you there Jason?

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:31.341
- [Jason] That's puke.

00:04:31.341 --> 00:04:32.748
(audience laughs)

00:04:32.748 --> 00:04:36.660
Yeah, that kinda stuff
happens whenever I take them

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.481
to do anything like, by myself.

00:04:38.481 --> 00:04:40.220
- Yes, of course.
- You know what I mean?

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.350
- Yeah, that's the time
they'll have diarrhea.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:43.183
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:04:43.183 --> 00:04:45.360
- So she threw up on me that day

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.800
and then the next time I took her,

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.710
we were like five miles from the house

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.880
and I started smelling something.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:52.713
- Hmm!

00:04:52.713 --> 00:04:54.530
- So I was like, all right,

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:56.700
maybe it's not that bad you know,

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.470
I get home and it's
coming out of the diaper.

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:00.450
- Yup.
- No!

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.560
- Into the car seat, which
anybody with kids knows

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:04.420
there's like a lot of
little nooks and crannies

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:05.253
in a car seat.

00:05:05.253 --> 00:05:06.760
- Yes, where Cheerio's hide, yeah!

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:09.420
- So you know, I'm
holding her out like this,

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:10.720
like trying to get her in the house.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.323
And then take this car seat
and use the water hose,

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.100
spray it off, it's you know.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.580
- You need a power
washer for that (laughs).

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:18.430
- I know, I know!

00:05:19.408 --> 00:05:21.790
- So is Memphis a big talker?

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:23.326
- He's starting to be a big talker.

00:05:23.326 --> 00:05:25.940
He's saying everything he hears right now,

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.237
it's his, yeah there he is.

00:05:27.237 --> 00:05:28.640
- [Kelly] That's my three year old, Remy.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:31.040
He repeats everything, so be careful.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:34.420
- I know trust me, I
say stuff and then he--

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.150
- Oh my gosh!
- He repeats it.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:37.757
- Do you just wanna eat him?

00:05:37.757 --> 00:05:38.590
(audience laughs)

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:39.840
He is so cute!

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.560
- That's actually been
a little while back.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.900
He's got like real, kinda longer hair now.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:44.733
- [Kelly] Oh my gosh!

00:05:44.733 --> 00:05:45.740
- [Jason] Yeah, he's awesome.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:47.680
- Big ole blue eyes, get it.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.940
So how do they react to your music?

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:50.790
- He loves it!

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:54.470
He came out, he's seen a couple shows.

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:56.740
He's usually in bed by
the time I play, so.

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:57.700
But I've had a couple things

00:05:57.700 --> 00:05:58.760
where he's been able to come out

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:02.450
and watch and you know, he just,

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:05.590
he's a little confused,
he's like, he's got a,

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:08.165
so when he was born, I
had a bunch of posters,

00:06:08.165 --> 00:06:10.970
pictures of people that I've worked with.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:12.885
I don't have you though,
but I should do that.

00:06:12.885 --> 00:06:13.718
(audience laughs)

00:06:13.718 --> 00:06:16.100
- Whatever, you're wearing
my shirt, it's even.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.980
- So it's like Luke Bryan
and George Strait and Miranda

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:20.190
and some of those people.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:23.360
And so, and I had them
sign big pictures to him

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.515
and one of 'em is from me
and I signed it to him.

00:06:25.515 --> 00:06:26.348
- That's adorable!

00:06:26.348 --> 00:06:29.080
- And so he looks at the
picture and he's like, dada,

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:31.720
and then he looks and me
and he's like dada too.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:34.100
Like he knows I'm the same person,

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.280
but he still hasn't
quite put it together so,

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:38.400
but they seem, I mean he
seems to be, you know,

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:39.700
into the music and you know,

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:41.840
you turn it on and he
knows it's me singing so.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:43.660
- It's funny when it starts to click.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:45.660
'Cause then he'll turn around and my kid,

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.480
will like, especially
River, she's five now.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.100
She'll turn around and
she'll hear people cheering

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.142
or whatever and she turns,
she goes, "That's my mom."

00:06:51.142 --> 00:06:52.720
(audience laughs)

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:55.070
I'm like, "Slow your roll."

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:56.990
Like she's like trying to
hit on the guys behind her.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:58.010
- At least she's proud of it.

00:06:58.010 --> 00:06:59.258
- I know!

00:06:59.258 --> 00:07:00.363
- She could be saying, I
don't know who that is.

00:07:00.363 --> 00:07:03.330
- I don't, yeah, that'll
come when she's 16.

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.030
But you also have two older daughters,

00:07:05.030 --> 00:07:07.606
that I've met and love,
are they musical at all?

00:07:07.606 --> 00:07:10.730
- My oldest daughter, Keeley
is, Keeley and Kendyl.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:13.180
My oldest one, Keeley,
has started playing guitar

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:14.850
and started wanting to play.

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.310
So I was like, all right
well I can teach you.

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:17.250
You know, you wanna play.

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:18.850
I mean I play rhythm guitar and I'm like,

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:20.850
you want to learn to play what I play?

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:22.075
And she's like, "No not really."

00:07:22.075 --> 00:07:23.735
(laughs)

00:07:23.735 --> 00:07:26.047
I said, "Well, what do you wanna know?"

00:07:26.047 --> 00:07:26.880
"How do you wanna play?"

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.030
And she's goes, "I wanna play like Slash

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.085
from Guns N' Roses."
(audience laughs)

00:07:30.085 --> 00:07:31.180
- Oh my God, she aims for the moon.

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:33.380
- Yeah and she's actually doing great man.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:35.436
She picked it up a lot
quicker than I thought

00:07:35.436 --> 00:07:38.510
and she's got another year of high school

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:40.330
and then you know, it's time for college,

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.610
so she wants to go do
something with music.

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:43.443
So we'll see,

00:07:43.443 --> 00:07:44.276
we'll see how it goes.
- Does that excite you?

00:07:44.276 --> 00:07:45.520
Or scare you?
- Both.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:46.353
Yeah, me too.
- You know,

00:07:46.353 --> 00:07:47.860
it's exciting that, you know,

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:50.710
that she kinda has the
bug and loves music.

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:52.700
- Yeah, and that kindred spirit,

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:54.946
but at the same time you've
like, oh, beware, yeah.

00:07:54.946 --> 00:07:56.348
- Yeah, it's just, you
know it's a tough business.

00:07:56.348 --> 00:07:57.181
- Yes!

00:07:57.181 --> 00:07:58.690
- You know and you gotta
have a pretty thick skin.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:07:59.660
- [Kelly] Yes!

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:02.060
- But you know, I love the
fact that she's picked it up

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.360
and, you know, just the fact
that she can play an instrument

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:05.260
to me is cool, so.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:06.758
- Yeah, oh my gosh.

00:08:06.758 --> 00:08:07.591
(audience applauds)

00:08:07.591 --> 00:08:09.849
Well you recently posted
a photo of your papa?

00:08:09.849 --> 00:08:10.690
- Yeah.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.360
- And I didn't know this story.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.270
But I didn't know that your
real last name wasn't Aldean.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:17.060
- [Jason] No, so my last name is Williams.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:18.530
- Okay, very different.

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:20.320
- But yeah so when I was in high school

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:22.740
and started playing music,

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:26.200
there was a couple basketball
players named Jason Williams.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:29.280
And so, and there was a
piano guy in Nashville,

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:30.610
named Jason D Williams,

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:32.750
so it was just so common you know.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:35.270
And so I started using my
middle name which was Aldean.

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:39.060
And my grandfather, who, I was
named after my grandfather.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:40.780
And so I had a cousin
in town the other day

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:44.040
and we started talking about him.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:47.750
And my uncle, years ago had started taking

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:49.550
my grandfather out and
he would just record,

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:50.540
you know, had a little recorder

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:53.590
and he would record conversations
and he put 'em on a CD.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.190
So I've been driving around in my truck,

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:58.570
listening to those CD's
and my cousin was in town

00:08:58.570 --> 00:08:59.450
and we were talking about him.

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:03.010
And so that night, I just
kinda, had the feels as you say,

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:04.810
and--
- Exactly, did you cry?

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:06.185
- No, I didn't.
- Judging you now.

00:09:06.185 --> 00:09:07.750
(audience laughs)
- I didn't.

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:09.850
But it's just cool I was named after him,

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:12.180
and it was a name growing
up that I was like,

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.740
man I was, whenever somebody
called me Jason Aldean,

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:16.050
it was usually 'cause I was in trouble.

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:16.883
- Yes.
(audience laughs)

00:09:16.883 --> 00:09:19.810
- So little did I know
that that would be the name

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:22.710
that everybody would know me
as, you know, from now on.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:25.780
And he didn't live long
enough to kinda see that.

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:28.100
You know, so, I was just
kinda thinking about him

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:32.371
a lot that night but then I
ended up naming my only son

00:09:32.371 --> 00:09:34.820
after him too Memphis Aldean, so yeah.

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:35.697
- It's such a cool name.
- Thank you.

00:09:35.697 --> 00:09:38.510
- Such a strong rock 'n roll name.

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:41.550
This is awesome and I love
that it's smoke, the 9.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:42.383
- [Jason] Yeah!

00:09:42.383 --> 00:09:43.880
- So 9, what's the significance?

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:45.479
- So 9 for me, this was an album--

00:09:45.479 --> 00:09:47.331
- I know it's you're 9th album but--

00:09:47.331 --> 00:09:49.520
- Yeah 9th album, but
for me this was an album

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:52.130
that years ago I just,
kinda it was a pipe dream,

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:54.450
but I always said if I
ever made nine records,

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:56.150
I was gonna call it 9 or number 9,

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:58.390
because I played a lot
of baseball growing up.

00:09:58.390 --> 00:10:00.370
I actually thought that
was gonna be my career path

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:03.660
for a long time and nine was my number

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:06.507
that I wore playing baseball
all those years and so--

00:10:06.507 --> 00:10:09.087
- Oh look at itty bitty Jason!

00:10:09.087 --> 00:10:10.730
Look at you!
- Oh, there you go.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:14.110
There you go, that was high
school and no facial hair.

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:15.480
Right there.
(audience laughs)

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:18.050
- But tell us about the album
and this is cool art too.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:19.150
So what is different?

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:21.730
'Cause I know I've made like eight by now,

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:23.320
but you start to go,

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.130
what do I do that's
different on this album,

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:26.466
so what do you think is different?

00:10:26.466 --> 00:10:27.580
- Well I think that's
the thing, is you know,

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:29.300
after making so many records,

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:32.100
you have to, you know,
kinda go in and get creative

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:33.600
and a lot of times you feel like, man,

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:35.260
I've already done this and
I've already done that.

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:38.168
And you know, for me, luckily, I mean,

00:10:38.168 --> 00:10:40.760
my influencers are all over the place.

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:42.070
I mean I'm from Macon, Georgia,

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:45.020
which is the home of the Allman
Brothers and Otis Redding

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:46.820
and Little Richard and, but you know,

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:49.140
songs for me like Dirt Road
Anthem and those kind of things.

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:52.596
I mean those came from me
liking different genres of music

00:10:52.596 --> 00:10:54.270
and it's kinda the same on this record.

00:10:54.270 --> 00:10:55.370
I mean I think for me,

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:56.590
I wanna go in and make albums

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:59.840
where there's gonna be some
traditional country songs,

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:00.840
because I love that stuff.

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:04.890
There's gonna be some, you
know, more R&amp;B type stuff

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:06.147
and rock 'n roll is a huge thing for me.

00:11:06.147 --> 00:11:08.710
- Anything, you know else,
like "You Make It Easy".

00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:09.623
- There is one!
- Okay!

00:11:09.623 --> 00:11:11.930
- There's a song on there
called "I Got What I Got".

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:12.870
- Okay I'm in.

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:14.830
- Yeah, so I think that'll be your jam.

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:17.168
- Spotify knows that I stalk you (laughs).

00:11:17.168 --> 00:11:18.860
(audience laughs)

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:21.147
They all are watching us, beware (laughs).

